One of these years I'll remember the usual fix.  Generally these are
server export permission problems.  What sort of system is the server,
what is in the exports file?

>While I'm out here begging for help, let me ask another one: How does
>one increase the size of the vnode table? This beast crashed once, with
>the "vnode table full" message. I changed the definition of NVNODE in
>param.c from
>#define NVNODE (NPROC + (2*MAXUSERS) + 128)
>(the ULTRIX definition) to
>#define NVNODE (((((2*NPROC)+ 16 + MAXUSERS) + 32)*2) + (NMOUNT+((8*NMOUNT)*2)))
>(the OSF/1 definition)

>Is this OK? The kernel built and runs without problems. 

We punted on the OSF definition because it makes so many more vnodes.  What
sort of applications are you running that open a lot of files?  How
many automount "intercept points" do you have (do "mount -e | grep '(pid' | wc".
Each mounted file system (including those automount intercepts) use up
two vnodes, and is often the culprit.  If the mount points are eating up
a lot, increasing NVNODE is okay.  Otherwise you may want to increase
maxusers, as that increases a lot other things, like file structs, number
or processes, etc.

>And one final question: Is there any utility to monitor kernel parameters?

ps, vmstat, iostat, netstat, nfsstat are things we ship, syd and top are
lurking around the Internet.  Also look into kdbx's extensions.

