All prototypes for all the socket calls were added in OSF/1 V2.0 

As an work around (All the usual disclaimers - this info is from Digital
internal infobase and not an official Digital recommendation; yata yata ...)

Use this code with your socket functions (extended appropriately for
any other socket functions you use):

extern "C"
{
    bzero(void *, int);
    gethostname(char *, int);
    struct servent *getservbyname(char *, char *);
    struct hostent *gethostbyname(char *);
    unsigned short htons(unsigned short);
    unsigned int htonl(unsigned int);
    /*
     * socket routines
     */
    accept(int, void *, int *);
    bind(int, void *, int);
    connect(int, void *, int);
    listen(int, int);
    recv(int, char *, int, int);
    select(int, fd_set *, fd_set *, fd_set *, struct timeval *);
    send(int, char *, int, int);
    setsockopt(int, int, int, char *, int);
    shutdown(int, int);
    socket(int, int, int);
}

Note that these function defs are not in socket.h, or any other
system header file, to my knowledge.  I'm no language expert, but I
think that K+R C will not complain about undefined functions, and
defaults them to returning int, and the args don't matter.  The
linker finds them and everything works.  C++ (and strict ansi C)
will complain about undefined functions so you get compile errors.

Good Luck!


