The quality of DEC's software products is really amazing...
I simplified the example a little bit:

------ crash.c: --------
int i(double x)
{
  int *p;
  p= &x;
  return *p;
}
------- EOF ------------

First of all, please note that that piece of code may even have
an application: If int and double have the same storage size, 
this should just 'typecast' the argument x to an int, yielding the internal 
representation of the double. You could then use the int to extract
the fields of the double (sign, mantissa, exponent).

However, a compilation attempt had the following result:
% cc -O crash.c
/usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/cfe: Warning: crash.c, line 4: Incompatible pointer type 
assignment
   p= &x;
 ---^
Fatal error in: /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ugen Segmentation fault - core dumped 

Our OS and compiler version:
% uname -a
OSF1 picard V1.3 112 alpha
% cc -V
cc  (cc)
        The DEC OSF/1 AXP Compiler Driver 3.11
[many $RCSfile: lines deleted]

 cc without "-O" also worked for me. I can't verify if it works on OSF1 1.2,
because I don't have it. "c89 -O" also core dumps (you must explicitly type-
cast &x to (int *) in order to make c89 compile the code), because it
invokes the same "ugen".

 I don't know if the bug (YES, this is a *BUG*) resides in the OS or the
compiler, but I hope the people at DEC (who are reading this, right?) will
*IMMEDIATELY* find that out and fix it. 

Greetings,
-Gerhard


