From:    Dr Marco Luchini <m.luchini@ic.ac.uk>

The following is the "official" line wrt 'lazy' vs 'eager' swap.
As a postscript I should add that it is somwhat pessimistic.  I've been
running 3 alphas for a year like that in an active research environment
and had no probs.  Just make sure that you sit hard on people who start
a 50Mb job without checking that there is space.  Shaming them by
pointing out that they are screwing everybody else up usually works
wonders.  

Regarding Jim's comment on what process gets killed, my understanding,
borne out in practice, is that the process to ask for more swap gets
killed.  Generally this is the shell with which you've just logged in
because things were becoming slow and  were about to solve the problem.
I keep an old window always open on my server just in case.  Old shells
never get killed.

The way you turn on lazy swap is

# mv /sbin/swapdefault /sbin/swapdefault-
# reboot

To go back to eager:

# mv /sbin/swapdefault- /sbin/swapdefault
# reboot

See Chapter 7 of the system and admin guide.


