In article <2gh6ah$gkq@quabbin.crl.dec.com>, jg@crl.dec.com (Jim Gettys) writes
:
|> Immediate swap space allocation means that the system allocates
|> swap space on disk at the time the process allocates more VM;
|> if there are any problems, that process gets notified,
|> and possibly terminated (by default).  This also means that the
|> total VM of all processes cannot exceed the available swap space.
|> 
|> "Lazy" or "deferred" allocation means that swap space is allocated
|> at the time the system actually needs to use more main memory for
|> some process (i.e. decides it has to page something out).  This
|> means that the total VM of all processes can equal the sum of
|> all available swap space + main memory.  This results in less disk
|> required to support the processes; you only need the amount of disk
|> space that is required to hold the VM being paged out.
|> 
|> The problem with "deferred" allocation is that at the time of allocation,
|> you don't know what process to penalize; the system must get memory
|> from somewhere (or would deadlock), so has to choose some process to
|> kill; and it isn't as smart as a person might be about what it chooses :-).
|> 
|> So lazy or deferred allocation is most suitable on single user systems
|> where you are the only person that would be affected; on a multiuser system
|> it is pretty unfriendly to have to terminate someone else's process for
|> reasons outside of their control.  And on production systems where you
|> want to guarantee that once a process starts, it will continue indefinately,
|> you'd better be using immediate allocation.  This is why we ship our OSF
|> with immediate allocation by default.
|> 				- Jim
|> 
