With all the discussions on eager swap mode for OSF/1 I started
wondering why every program for OSF/1 claims 8MB before it has
started. The only reason I can think of is that there is space
reserved in case a process writes to the shared library pages. If that
is correct (DEC?) there could probably be a reasonable intermediate
between eager and lazy swap, which does not allow Copy-On-Write for
textpages and thus saves about 8MB per process: just give a SIGSEGV
when a program tries to change text (this is probably already the
case). 

Currently the system I'm using is running lazy swap, and since I have
used the linux operating system for a long time already, I'm used to
that. Some people, however, might prefer an earlier warning. The
earger swap is a bit overdone. 

