First: as a point of nomenclature, the problem is not with sprintf
under OSF/1, as the subject says.  It's with sprintf on an Alpha, i.e.
it's a 64-bit porting problem.  You'd have the same problem porting
your application to an Alpha running VMS or NT, and you wouldn't have
it porting to another platform running OSF/1.

As for the sprintf problem: the -taso option will only work around the
most common coding-portability mistake, which is assuming a pointer
will fit in an integer and vice versa.  If your code assumes that a
long is the same size as an int, -taso can do nothing about that.  In
particular, when using *printf the following two (incorrect)
constructs will work correctly on a platform where
sizeof(int)==sizeof(long):

	printf("%d", <long>);
	printf("%ld", <int>);

The print routines examine the format string to determine the sizes to
expect for subsequent arguments.  On most machines ints and longs are
both 32 bits and the above, though sloppy, still works.  But on an
Alpha the first printf will be looking for a 32-bit (int-sized)
quantity and get 64, whereas the second will look for 64 and get 32.
I'd guess that your problems come from this.

Take a look at the lint(1) man page on an Alpha and look up the -Q
option.  Pay particular attention to the 'f' suboption.  Running

	lint -Q<everything-but-f> *.[ch]

or similar on your application should show where format string
mismatches occur.
