 Consider the following little C and Fortran programs:
--- 4711.c -------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

void main()
{
  int i;
  for(i= 1; i <= 4000; i++)
    printf("4711.47114711\n");
}
------------------------------------
This just prints the number 4711.47114711 to stdout 4000 times and then 
terminates.

--- crunch.f -----------------------
      program crunch

      integer i
      double precision a

      do 4711 i= 1, 4000
        read *, a
4711  continue

      print *, 'read 4000 numbers'

      end
------------------------------------
This trys to read 4000 numbers from stdin, and, if successfully, prints
the message 'read 4000 numbers' to stdout and terminates.

 So what would you expect if you connect those two programs by a pipe, e.g.
"4711 | crunch"? Well, 4711 should produce 4000 numbers, crunch should con-
sume them and happily terminate with 'read 4000 numbers'.
 And that's exactly what it does on a good old Tektronix m88k workstation.

