Well, I applaud the effort, but I have many qualms about the implementation...

I guess I expected DEC to come out with something remotely similar to other
vendors providing more advanced non-ufs fs's, and at least match a majority
of the useful features, while providing their own enhancements.

But after playing with it for a few days, I'm severely disappointed.  Yes,
it gives dec an easy way to have mega-big fs's and stuff, and supposedly
it's faster, but is a management nightmare...


The machine is a  DEC 3000/500X, 2 RZ26's, 2 RZ28's, 96MB's RAM. OSF 1.3A,
ADVFS 1.01 or whatever the last release on the Nov. CD's was.


For whatever brain-dead marketing reason, you get a bare-bones worthless
capability of creating an advfs on a single disk, or a logical volume,
or disk partition.  Of course, anything you want to do with multiple
volumes requires more licenses.  WHich sucks.  I think IBM provides this
functionality with the BOS.  Go figure.


1)  File domains work weirdly.  I created a LV of 2 disks [RZ28's] (since I 
don't have a license to be able to use the advfs on 2 drives w/o lvm).  Then
I created a home_dmn on top of this LV.  Then I created 3 filesets in
the domain, thinking it would be like having 3 separate disk drives.  Well,
it is and it isn't.  To wit:

# df       
Filesystem                512-blks        used       avail capacity  Mounted on
home_dmn#home_u0_fs       6291456      216528     6074928     3%    /home/spd/u0
home_dmn#home_u1_fs       6291456      216528     6074928     3%    /home/spd/u1
home_dmn#home_u2_fs       6291456      216528     6074928     3%    /home/spd/u2

For reasons I have not figured out, you can not create a fileset with
a specific size, all the filesets share a common pool of storage.  The *only*
mechanism you have to control file system growth is through quotas, which
is a major pain in the butt.

2)  You can't change the size of an advfs on the fly.  

If I change the size of the LV that the advfs is created on, the file system
doesn't get larger.  So it looks like to extend a file system even
with the so-called "advanced" features, you're still stuck in a dump/restore
cycle.  (Or rather vdump/vrestore with the advfs).

3)  The advfs/lvm combo is incapable of doing any "striping".  If you
buy another additional license, you can stripe a single file, but not
force an advfs to stripe the data across all the volumes in the domain.

4)  If you use advfs w/o lvm, I don't see anyway to do any mirroring, 
except by doing some kind of evil "clonefset" or somesuch.  Seems weird
to me.

5)  Not well integrated.

I just removed the lv that my domain was on, and the lv manager let me,
w/o warning me that there was a file domain/set active on it.  Now, I 
can't remove the domain/fileset, because the lv it was on doesn't
exist anymore.  

6)  Bizarro 4096/100 limits.  I can't even imagine where this comes from.
You can have 4096 file domains/sets, but only 100 mounted at 1 time.

7)  Bizarro naming scheme:  "domain"#"fileset"?  Sheesh, why not just make
it a dev in /dev, and be done with it.

8)  Evil licensing scheme.  advfs isn't even remotely useful w/o the 
advfs utilities, which are only available with YAL.


I guess I expected DEC to match minimal functionality of something like
IBM's jfs, with it's ability to grow on the fly, and tight integration
with the lvm.  DEC fails miserably in this category.

I'm hoping somebody from DEC comes along and says "wait, you missed
frobitz, which solves all your problems and does these other 91 cool
things".

I hope I'm wrong about some of the above stuff, I work for a fairly large
company that signed a big deal with EDS to do a bunch of equipment
stuff worldwide.  I'd dearly love it to be DEC, and I'm pushing it
that way as hard as I can, but after looking at this, it give me pause...
