From what I understand, the mirroring or shadowing will be available in the
"next release" untill then, you can use lvm, but that is where I would draw
the line with lvm use.  I found the clone file sets quite useful.  What you
get is a file set that is nothing more than a bunch of pointers to the 
original file set.  Any time a file is changed in either file set, the
block in the file that was touched is duplicated, and inserted into the 
chain of blocks for the changed file, but leaving the chain for the untouched
file intact.  This allows duplication of large active file-sets with very
little space or time consumed.  You then have a static copy of the file set 
(for backup or data fixing or whatever) and the first file set is on-line
being modified.  Blocks from the domain will only be consumed as they are
modified from the original file-set.  It's actually useful (and cool I think...)

