   [stuff deleted]

   1)  File domains work weirdly.  I created a LV of 2 disks [RZ28's] (since I 
   don't have a license to be able to use the advfs on 2 drives w/o lvm).  Then
   I created a home_dmn on top of this LV.  Then I created 3 filesets in
   the domain, thinking it would be like having 3 separate disk drives.  Well,
   it is and it isn't.  To wit:

   # df       
   Filesystem                512-blks        used       avail capacity  Mounted on
   home_dmn#home_u0_fs       6291456      216528     6074928     3%    /home/spd/u0
   home_dmn#home_u1_fs       6291456      216528     6074928     3%    /home/spd/u1
   home_dmn#home_u2_fs       6291456      216528     6074928     3%    /home/spd/u2

   For reasons I have not figured out, you can not create a fileset with
   a specific size, all the filesets share a common pool of storage.  The *only*
   mechanism you have to control file system growth is through quotas, which
   is a major pain in the butt.

See if you have the chfsets command.  If so, it can be used to change
the size (place a limit) on each fileset.  This command is not documented
through dxbook, so I missed it too at first.  I have a later release (beta
test) of ADVFS, so I'm not sure if the command is there in your release.  It
is part of the base operating system.

Beware two major bugs with early releases: 1.  a bug in nfs_server.o
(kernel module) which corrupts directors removed from ultrix clients
with the rm -r <dir>/ command.  The key here is the trailing "/".
This affects both ufs and advfs filesystems, but at least with ufs,
there is fsck to clean up the damage.  Every time advfs touches such a
corrupted directory, it hangs.  Due to retries, this will hang all
your nfsds if an nfs client tries to access such a corrupted
directory.  I've received a patched nfs_server.o from DEC, a patch to
advfs which prevents the hanging on a corrupted filesystem, and a
program which fixes the damage.  I'm told all this made it into DEC
OSF V2.0, but the bug is present on all external releases prior to
that.

2.  If you do the following on advfs you will hang it:

mkdir foo; cd foo; mkdir foo; mv foo ..

What this should do is generate the error message "mv: rename foo to
../foo: File exists."  Do it over nfs, and you will hang all the nfsds
on the server.  I've received a bug fix for this too, but I don't
think it made it into the V2.0 release.

Before I close, I'd like to say that the ADVFS crew at DEC have been
great at working with me to fix these bugs.  

-Henry


